From: fish <lunarisfelix02@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 01:10:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] op7t-0215: iOS vtable layout (no reserved slots) for Apple's kexts

Goal (option 1): load Apple's own USB device kexts from the iPhone X
iOS 16.5 kernelcache (AppleUSBCommon -> IOUSBDeviceFamily ->
AppleUSBDeviceMux) so the PC sees a real iPhone. Survey (tools/
kc_imports.py with a fixed __PRELINK_INFO parser, tools/kc_symcheck.py):
the three kexts reach 405 kernel symbols, all present in my kernel, and
nothing unnamed. But the vtables differ: OSObject 15 entries in the iOS
kernel vs 39 in ours, IOService 168 vs 272. By name the only difference
is 128 reserved padding slots (_RESERVEDOSMetaClassBase*, _RESERVEDOSObject*,
_RESERVEDIOService*): iOS kernels are built with APPLE_KEXT_VTABLE_PADDING
0, ours (macOS SDK, XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX) with 1, so every virtual call in an
iOS kext would hit the wrong slot.

Padding off for the sweet board. All C++ in my kernel is rebuilt with it;
the closed KDK library members I link are C/asm only (no vtables).
APPLE_KEXT_ALIGN_CONTAINERS stays 1 (macOS arm64 rule = iOS rule here).

Apply after 0214.
---
diff --git a/libkern/libkern/c++/OSMetaClass.h b/libkern/libkern/c++/OSMetaClass.h
--- a/libkern/libkern/c++/OSMetaClass.h
+++ b/libkern/libkern/c++/OSMetaClass.h
@@ -162,7 +162,12 @@ class OSInterface
 
 #ifdef XNU_KERNEL_PRIVATE
 
-#if !XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX
+/*
+ * op7t 0215: the sweet kernel is built with the macOS SDK (XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX) but must host Apple's iOS 16
+ * arm64 kexts (IOUSBDeviceFamily, AppleUSBDeviceMux, AppleUSBCommon from the iPhone X kernelcache). iOS
+ * kernels have no reserved vtable slots: same layout as theirs, every virtual call slot matches.
+ */
+#if !XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX || defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
 #define APPLE_KEXT_VTABLE_PADDING   0
 #else /* !XNU_TARGET_OS_OSX */
 #define APPLE_KEXT_VTABLE_PADDING   1
